A thread that runs on spare time past its request costs its virtual time nothing.
It would come back from a long run on spare time as far behind as a thread that waited, and be due as early;
a turn on its own units' place moves its virtual time on past its request too, up to a span past the clock.

Caught-by: fuzz fuzz-pmp8 fuzz-rp2350 fuzz-smp2 fuzz-work qemu-replay
Report: a step of the clock did not charge the thread whose turn it was its units' part
Report: a traced call that moved no time charged the thread whose turn it was
Witness: fuzz bind-turn-later

diff --git a/kernel/sched.c b/kernel/sched.c
--- a/kernel/sched.c
+++ b/kernel/sched.c
@@ -372,7 +372,7 @@ static uint64_t turn_vtime(const struct core *core, const struct place *p, uint6
     if (own == NULL || units == 0 || now <= core->turn_from) {
         return p->vt;
     }
-    uint64_t bound = p == own ? now + place_span(p) : p->vt;
+    uint64_t bound = p->vt;
     /* What is left of the request open on p, from one count to its span, so 0 for none. */
     uint32_t room = 0;
